---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site-config';
import { SITES } from '../lib/sites';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.main}/export/`;
const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.main}/` },
    { name: 'Export', url: canonical },
  ]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="Charcoal Export & Logistics"
  description="Charcoal export support — documentation, container loading and destination-port planning for international buyers."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Export', url: '/export/' }]}
>
  <div class="page-title container">
    <h1>Export &amp; Logistics</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Export support for international charcoal buyers — documentation, container loading and
      destination-port planning. Figures here describe industry practice and public supplier data,
      not guarantees for any specific order.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What We Support</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <div class="card"><h3>Documentation</h3><p>Commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin and export documents.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Container Loading</h3><p>Optimized loading to maximize weight and protect the product.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Destination Planning</h3><p>Port and delivery planning for your market.</p></div>
      </div>

      <h2>Container Loading Reference</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          How much charcoal fits a container depends on product form, packaging and packing density,
          so a single figure never applies to every load. As a planning reference drawn from public
          supplier data for coconut shell hookah charcoal, a 20-foot container loads approximately
          <strong>14.4 tonnes</strong> and a 40-foot high-cube approximately <strong>23.4 tonnes</strong>.
          Retail-packaged goods and palletised loads typically load lighter than bulk bags, because
          boxes and pallets consume volume.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Container</th><th>Indicative load (coconut shell briquettes)</th><th>Note</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>20 ft GP</td><td class="spec-value">~14.4 t</td><td>Public supplier data; varies with packaging</td></tr>
            <tr><td>40 ft HQ</td><td class="spec-value">~23.4 t</td><td>Public supplier data; varies with packaging</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="updated">
        Indicative figures only — the load plan for a specific order is confirmed with the supplier
        and freight forwarder before booking.
      </p>

      <h2>Ports of Loading</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Common Chinese ports of loading for charcoal exports are <strong>Shanghai</strong>,
          <strong>Ningbo</strong> and <strong>Shenzhen</strong>. The practical choice depends on the
          supplier's location and the shipping line serving your destination. Charcoal is a
          dangerous good (UN 1361, Class 4.2, self-heating), so the carrier and the loading port
          must accept Class 4.2 cargo — a detail worth confirming before committing to a route.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Export Documentation</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>A typical export set for a charcoal shipment includes:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Commercial Invoice (CI)</strong> — value and terms of the sale.</li>
          <li><strong>Packing List (PL)</strong> — quantities, weights and packaging per carton/pallet.</li>
          <li><strong>Bill of Lading (BL)</strong> — the carrier's receipt and title document.</li>
          <li><strong>Certificate of Origin (CO)</strong> — states the country of origin, often required for duty treatment.</li>
          <li><strong>Fumigation Certificate</strong> — required where the destination or packing material (e.g. wooden pallets) demands phytosanitary treatment.</li>
          <li><strong>Dangerous Goods Declaration &amp; MSDS</strong> — required because charcoal ships under UN 1361, Class 4.2.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The exact document set is destination-specific and is confirmed per order with the
          buyer's broker. For the classification and compliance side, see the
          <a href="/compliance/">compliance notes</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Lead Time</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          For OEM or printed private-label orders, an indicative lead time of
          <strong>15–25 days after artwork approval</strong> is commonly cited in public supplier
          listings. This is industry practice, not a contractual commitment: production, packaging
          print runs and vessel availability all move the real schedule. Standard stock-packaged
          orders typically move faster because no print or tooling step is involved.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cta-banner">
        <h2>Planning an import?</h2>
        <p>Tell us your destination port and volume — we'll confirm the load plan and document set.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/request-quote/">Request a Quote →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
